ows","change":"Charleston's days and nights both warmed. Nights warmed faster.","rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 65\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>65\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 69\u00b0F, and the record is 82\u00b0F (1989). The low of <strong>45\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Charleston has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Charleston's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Charleston has set <strong class=\"sig\">37 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 109 that chance alone would give, and <strong>121 record highs</strong>, close to the 109 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (56). Charleston's all-time high is 105\u00b0F, on August 1, 1999.","t_chg":"Charleston's days and nights both warmed. Nights warmed faster.","p_chg":"Across 88 complete years: Charleston's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.48\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.38 to +0.58), about +4.2\u00b0F since 1938. Charleston's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.24\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +0.33), about +2.1\u00b0F since 1938.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 11 of 12 months. Highs rose in 7 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Charleston's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.22 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 52.5 inches a year. The wettest year was 2015 (74.89 in); the driest, 1954 (30.31 in). Charleston almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.4 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 14 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows"}}